    {
      "id": "week-in-review-safer-storage-portable-playlists-local-voice",
      "title": "A week of safer storage, portable playlists and home-aware voice",
      "dek": "Last week connected safeguards against storage failures, ways to move music between services, and local speech recognition tailored to the household.",
      "body": [
        "From Monday, August 31 through Sunday, September 6, three threads stood out across the open-home ecosystem: protecting a home’s installation against failures, moving music collections between services, and helping local voice systems recognize the names people actually use.",
        "Home Assistant tackled storage failures at two layers. Supervisor’s network-share redesign keeps an unreachable server from freezing system services or diverting writes onto local storage. Separately, Home Assistant OS makes downloaded container-image data durable before treating it as complete, protecting it against a power cut. Stefan Agner’s changes cover both the storage outside the box and the software inside it.",
        "Music Assistant connected playlist copying with track recovery. A destination picker and background migration move eligible static playlists between supported providers, while matching on import replaces confirmed-dead entries using other connected services. Temporarily unavailable providers are left alone. The combination makes collections more portable without treating a service outage as a reason to replace their tracks.",
        "OHF Voice approached household vocabulary from two directions. Its Whisper service gives likely spoken names and their aliases priority within a limited recognition prompt. The new Speech-to-Phrase Home Assistant app instead builds a restricted set of recognizable commands from entities, rooms and actions. These are different recognition approaches, but both draw on knowledge of the home to narrow what the system needs to listen for.",
        "The week’s highlights strengthen three everyday foundations: resilient storage, a music library that can move between services, and local voice control that draws on the household’s own names."
      ],
      "kind": "weekly_recap",
      "placement": "feature",
      "score": 85,
